  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two hydrologic settings in a fractured rocky basin and how they affect magnesium carbonate formation. In the upper scene, an active hydrologic cycle with rainfall, surface water, and shoreline or delta input brings in mixed sediments and dissolved material. A standing water body occupies the basin, and dissolved magnesium and carbonate remain in the lake water, while groundwater also moves through fractures below. This panel suggests a relatively open, wetter system where material is transported and dispersed.\n\nThe lower scene shows a drier setting where evaporation exceeds precipitation. Here the water body is reduced and the groundwater table is emphasized. Under these conditions, magnesium carbonate appears to precipitate directly within fractures and also accumulate in basin-floor terraces. The central deposit is shown as evaporite-rich sediment, with magnesium carbonate present as cement and concretions. Compared with the upper panel, the lower one highlights concentration of dissolved ions by evaporation, followed by mineral precipitation in subsurface cracks and near-surface sediments.\n\nOverall, the figure illustrates how shifting from a wetter lake system to an evaporation-dominated basin promotes localized mineral enrichment and MgCO3 deposition.",
